Z||000000||GT Bastard||11-13-2000||06:49 PM||||Just wondering what everyone else paid to have their engine balanced by a machine shop.  It's a 3.2 stoker. <P>-Jeff||||reg||4
Z||000001||Tom88gt||11-13-2000||07:26 PM||||It should add about $100 to the cost.  Never hand it done yet, never rebuilt an engine yet.  But PAW's adds $100 to their engines for balancing.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Shaun41178||11-13-2000||10:16 PM||||I payed $200 to have my assembly balanced.  The pistons were already balanced and matched from the factory and were within .25 gram if I am not mistaken.  I had the harmonic Dampener, crankshaft, pistons and rings, rods, bearings and neutral flexplate balanced as a complete assembly.  Good thing I had too cause my machinist said that the front of the crank was off 14 grams and the back was off 14 grams as well.  I can't even imagine how bad it would have shaken if not balanced.  Its all down to less then a half gram on the front and back now.<P>I definately would not skimp on having the assembly balanced.  It will allow increased rpms and get a tad bit more power too.<P><P>------------------<BR>Shaun-Toledo OH, 85 GT<BR>3.2 With nitrous<BR>http://members.aol.com/shaun41178||||reg||1
Z||000003||fiero56||11-13-2000||10:17 PM||||Hey Jeff,<P>What did you pay to have the crank ground? Wanna' know if I'm getting screwed or not.||||reg||1
